e=runpy.run_path(str(ROOT/'writer-middle/compose.py'))
q,obs,part=e['q'],e['obs'],e['part'];base=e['base']
if D=='2025-08-18':
 lead={'title':'Opendoor / OPEN — the crowd wins the leadership argument','parts':[
 part('Why Retail Is Here',"Opendoor turns a difficult housing business into a story the crowd can understand: pressure the leadership, change the direction and let a recovery in housing do the rest. CNBC reports that the chief executive resigned following an investor pressure campaign. [[n5159]] That is a concrete event around which a speculative constituency can organise. The attraction is agency as much as valuation: shareholders can tell themselves they helped force the reset. A large move then becomes social proof that the argument is working. The temptation is to move directly from a change in leadership to a change in enterprise value. Those are different steps. A new management framework can improve the chance of a repair without immediately changing property turnover, financing costs or cash consumption."),
 part('What Is Driving It',obs('OPEN',(1,5,20),'Opendoor')+"The size of the advance makes this an expectations trade as well as a corporate transition. Business Insider describes the renewed surge after July's meme-like rally. [[n5077]] The crowd can interpret renewed strength as evidence that the earlier burst was the opening act rather than the entire opportunity. But a market that has already repriced the possibility of change needs delivery to justify the next leg. Housing finance and transaction activity determine whether an operating improvement can catch up with the share price. A leadership catalyst is real; a profitable housing recovery remains a test. Keep the trading position separate from an assumption that the underlying business has already been repaired."),
 part('The Social Signal',"RealEstateNews describes activists claiming victory as the chief executive steps down. [[n5128]] Bloomberg separately reported that the shares rallied as crypto influencer Pompliano bought stock. [[n4906]] Those are identifiable channels of attention: an activist victory narrative and an influential buyer, rather than an invented count of online posts. The story invites participation because it connects a visible company event to the belief that a loosely organised constituency can shape an outcome. That can reinforce demand for the shares even while the fundamental repair is incomplete. It also makes disagreement feel personal. A skeptical assessment of margins or funding can be treated as opposition to the movement, which is precisely when investors need a written exit rule rather than a loyalty test."),
 part('Historical Analog',"The mechanical comparison is with the participation loop associated with GameStop 2021, rather than a claim that the companies have the same economics. A crowd identifies an apparent outsider, gives a catalyst symbolic meaning and reads subsequent price strength as validation of its collective judgment. Business Insider's description of Opendoor's meme-like rally supplies the present-day link. [[n5077]] The important similarity is that a shareholder becomes a participant in the story, making it harder to distinguish an investment decision from a declaration of belief. The analogy is useful only as a risk framework: a self-reinforcing constituency can extend a move, but it does not provide a floor when marginal buyers change their minds. The housing business still has to earn its financing."),
 part('Smart Money View',"The institutional question is whether a governance reset improves the expected return on capital. The activist victory reported by RealEstateNews establishes a changed leadership situation, not an agreed valuation or a completed business turnaround. [[n5128]] An investor can welcome accountability while waiting for evidence on cash generation, asset turnover and funding. The broader warning comes from the Financial Times account of investors losing billions as meme-stock pump-and-dump scams multiply; that is a warning about a class of market behaviour, not an allegation about Opendoor. [[n5217]] Keep that scope clear. Professionals should judge the business and the financing structure while acknowledging that attention can move the price faster than either. A credible repair and an overheated entry price can coexist."),
 part('When the Narrative Breaks',"The first failure would be a leadership reset that does not produce a credible operating plan. A second would be housing conditions that remain too difficult for the company to turn inventory and fund activity on attractive terms. The share price can also fail before either operating test is settled if the constituency that bought the symbolic victory takes profits. CNBC's account of the resignation makes governance the immediate catalyst; it does not promise a financing improvement. [[n5159]] Use a measured cash-equity position and reassess if the rally stops responding to constructive company developments. Avoid borrowing against a thesis whose short-term support comes partly from attention. The exit should be the loss of a workable operating or market mechanism, rather than the desire to prove that the crowd was right.") ]}
 smaller=[
 {'title':'Intel / INTC — strategic support becomes an equity option','parts':[part('Why Retail Is Here',q(r'Intel.*(?:stake|Trump)|(?:stake|Trump).*Intel','retail')+obs('INTC',(5,20),'Intel')+"The hook is strategic relevance: if policymakers regard domestic chip capability as important, a troubled company can look less isolated. That is an option on financing and industrial policy, not proof that manufacturing economics have improved. The attraction is strongest when a difficult turnaround acquires a recognisable outside sponsor."),part('Where It Breaks',q(r'Intel.*stake|stake.*Intel')+"A proposed or possible government stake must remain a possibility until terms are agreed. Shareholders need to know the price, dilution, governance and the capital still required. Policy support can reduce one kind of risk while leaving execution risk in place. Prefer a small equity position over an assumption that strategic importance guarantees a favourable shareholder return. A financing arrangement that disadvantages existing owners, or a failure to convert support into customer demand, would undermine the trade.")]},
 {'title':'Ether / ETH — demand is real but wrappers add another wager','parts':[part('Why Retail Is Here',q(r'Ethereum.*(?:near record|ETF|treasury)|ETH.*(?:ETF|treasury)','crypto')+obs('ETH-USD',(5,20),'Ether')+"The simple narrative is that more investable access creates more potential buyers. Retail can express that directly through the token or indirectly through an equity vehicle, but those choices should not be treated as interchangeable. A company buying tokens also brings financing terms, operating expenses and dilution into the return."),part('Where It Breaks',q(r'Ethereum treasury.*(?:loss|stock)|BitMine.*(?:offering|raise)|Bitmine.*Fundraising','crypto')+"The narrative weakens if funding becomes harder or an equity premium contracts even while the underlying token holds up. Prefer the direct asset when the thesis is token demand; use a treasury equity only when its per-share financing economics are attractive. A higher gross token balance does not automatically mean a higher economic claim for each existing shareholder. Keep leverage modest enough to survive a normal reversal in a market that trades through the weekend.")]},
 {'title':'CoreWeave / CRWV — believers buy the financing test','parts':[part('Why Retail Is Here',q(r'CoreWeave.*(?:loss|losses|shares drop|revenue)','earnings')+obs('CRWV',(5,20),'CoreWeave')+"A sharp drawdown can attract investors who see the same AI demand story at a cheaper price. The seductive argument is that the market is focusing on the cost of expansion while missing the scale of the opportunity. That argument needs evidence that funded capacity will earn an adequate return."),part('Where It Breaks',q(r'CoreWeave.*(?:lockup|lock.up|investor|sell|cost)','retail')+"The distinction is between a temporary expectations reset and a business whose financing needs keep rising faster than distributable earnings. Capital intensity, shareholder supply and customer concentration can matter even while demand grows. Own a limited equity position only if the financing path supports the operating thesis. If a lower price is accompanied by a less attractive capital structure, averaging down can increase exposure to the very problem that caused the decline.")]}
 ]
 base['retail_intro']='Opendoor is the participation story in focus, with the reflexive appeal associated with GameStop 2021, Tesla 2020 and Bitcoin 2020–21: the crowd sees a visible catalyst and wants ownership in the next chapter.'
 base['retail']=[lead]+smaller
elif D=='2025-08-19':
 lead={'title':'Intel / INTC — a real sponsor changes the turnaround debate','parts':[
 part('Why Retail Is Here',"Intel now offers a more concrete rescue narrative than a vague appeal to strategic importance. CNBC reports that SoftBank is making a $2 billion investment. [[n4290]] Retail can read that as outside validation from a recognisable technology investor: someone with capital is prepared to participate in the turnaround. The hook is that a former industry leader might acquire the financing and attention needed for another chapter. That does not settle the return available to ordinary shareholders, but it changes the conversation from whether support might appear to what a named investment can accomplish. The emotional attraction is the possibility of buying a troubled franchise before its operating reputation improves. The discipline is to keep that possibility separate from a completed manufacturing recovery."),
 part('What Is Driving It',obs('INTC',(1,5,20),'Intel')+"The closing price and the investment headline answer different questions: the price shows the risk already being carried, while the announcement creates a new financing issue to evaluate. CNN also reports the SoftBank investment. [[n4294]] Do not manufacture a share-price reaction by attaching the daily move mechanically to the latest announcement. The operating hurdle remains the ability to turn funded capacity and customer demand into acceptable returns. An investor with a short horizon may trade the sponsorship story; an investor underwriting a business recovery needs evidence about execution and capital productivity. Those positions can coexist, but they require different sizing and different reasons to exit."),
 part('The Social Signal',"The popular narrative has an obvious anchor in a named backer rather than a rumour about anonymous interest. CNBC's investment report supplies that anchor. [[n4290]] The separate BBC account of a possible US stake makes strategic support part of the surrounding debate, but it remains a possibility. [[n6357]] The crowd can combine the two into a powerful belief that important institutions will not let the company fail. That is an interpretation of the headlines, not evidence of a measured online consensus or a guarantee to shareholders. The attention risk is that survival becomes confused with attractive equity economics. A company can remain strategically important while the price paid, the funding terms and the time required still make the investment difficult."),
 part('Historical Analog',"The useful analogy is the second-wave participation seen around large technology turnaround narratives, with Tesla 2020 and the enthusiasm surrounding Nvidia 2023 serving as familiar examples of how investors can extrapolate a changed story into a much larger future. The mechanical point is the arrival of a validating event: a recognised participant makes a commitment, late buyers treat it as permission to revisit a difficult stock and price momentum can then attract another constituency. Here the validating event is the named SoftBank investment reported by CNN. [[n4294]] The analogy does not imply similar products, margins or future returns. Its value is to expose the inference investors are tempted to make: outside sponsorship can reduce uncertainty, but it cannot replace the work required to earn a return on the capital."),
 part('Smart Money View',"A professional investor should ask what the investment changes in the company's financing capacity and what obligations remain. Reuters describes the possibility of a government stake as a report-driven catalyst, which is a different level of certainty from a completed policy package. [[n8408]] SoftBank's participation is a concrete development, but it is not evidence that every other potential supporter will act on favourable terms. The constructive case is that additional capital and credibility improve the options available to management. The skeptical case is that a capital-intensive turnaround can consume repeated funding before producing attractive shareholder returns. Neither view requires pretending that all institutional investors agree. The relevant debate is about terms, execution and time, rather than a simple distinction between believers and doubters."),
 part('When the Narrative Breaks',"The immediate failure would be support whose economics are less attractive to existing shareholders than the popular interpretation assumes. A further risk is that a better financing position does not translate into stronger customer economics or credible execution. Reuters' possible-stake headline should stay conditional; it is not a commitment to a completed government transaction. [[n8408]] Use a measured equity position where the financing catalyst is worth the operating uncertainty, and reduce it if the narrative expands faster than the evidence. The crowd's strongest argument is that an important sponsor has arrived. The clean invalidation is that sponsorship fails to improve the expected return on the next unit of capital. Strategic significance can keep a business relevant without putting a floor under its ordinary shares.") ]}
 smaller=[
 {'title':'Nuclear suppliers / URA — the power customer becomes visible','parts':[part('Why Retail Is Here',q(r'Google, Kairos Power plan advanced nuclear|Morgan Stanley says nuclear power','retail')+"The attraction is a recognisable electricity customer attached to an otherwise distant technology story. Retail can imagine a chain from AI computing to more power and then to nuclear investment. The customer link matters, but it must be mapped carefully: a project involving one developer does not create an order for every listed nuclear company."+obs('URA',(5,),'Uranium equity ETF')),part('Where It Breaks',q(r'Morgan Stanley says nuclear power|Google, Kairos Power plan advanced nuclear','retail')+"A uranium equity fund, a reactor developer and a regulated utility carry different commercial risks. None should be described as a direct beneficiary of a contract it does not hold. The thesis weakens if licensing, financing or delivery turns the appealing demand narrative into a long wait for cash. Prefer funded, identifiable exposure; keep speculative development risk smaller than the headline excitement would suggest.")]},
 {'title':'UnitedHealth / UNH — a famous buyer offers permission to revisit','parts':[part('Why Retail Is Here',q(r'Buffett.*UnitedHealth|Burry.*UnitedHealth','retail')+obs('UNH',(5,20),'UnitedHealth')+"A respected investor's purchase can make an unpopular franchise easier for a retail account to reconsider. The emotional hook is borrowing confidence from someone with a patient reputation. That is a reason to investigate the operating repair, not proof that the investor's cost, time horizon or risk budget matches a new buyer's situation."),part('Where It Breaks',q(r'UnitedHealth.*recovery|Buffett.*UnitedHealth','retail')+"The business still needs a credible path through medical costs, pricing and regulatory uncertainty. A filing establishes ownership at a historical point; it does not reveal every subsequent decision or guarantee agreement on the current price. Keep exposure tied to an improving operating case. If costs or cash generation deteriorate, a famous shareholder's name is not an adequate substitute for an exit decision.")]},
 {'title':'Ether / ETH — the pullback tests the new believers','parts':[part('Why Retail Is Here',q(r'Bitcoin Price, XRP, Ethereum Fall|Bitcoin, ethereum slip as crypto markets pull back','crypto')+obs('ETH-USD',(5,20),'Ether')+"The fresh question is whether investors who liked the expansion will still provide demand when the price retreats. That shifts attention from the excitement of access and treasury buying to the resilience of the marginal buyer. A pullback can be an entry opportunity or the beginning of a loss of conviction; its size alone does not answer which."),part('Where It Breaks',q(r'SEC.*(?:Punts|delays).*Ethereum|Ethereum.*treasury.*loss|Ethereum.*(?:ETF|treasury)','crypto')+"Policy timing and the financing of corporate wrappers remain separate risks. Prefer a direct token expression when the thesis is asset demand, and require attractive per-share economics before paying for a treasury company. The changed angle is resilience during a reversal, rather than another celebration of rising prices. Reduce leverage if liquidity deteriorates, because a continuously traded asset can move while the equity wrapper is unavailable for trading.")]}
 ]
 base['retail_intro']='Intel brings a named sponsor to the turnaround story, with the validation loop familiar from Tesla 2020, Nvidia 2023 and the earlier internet investment cycle: outside commitment invites a second wave of believers.'
 base['retail']=[lead]+smaller

elif D=='2025-08-20':
 lead={'title':'Palantir / PLTR — the growth story meets a valuation revolt','parts':[
 part('Why Retail Is Here',"Palantir's attraction is a simple claim on enterprise AI: own the software business that turns a vast technology opportunity into something customers can use. CNBC's description of its 'astronomical growth' captures the language around which that belief can form. [[n4968]] The popular thesis is that an exceptional operating franchise deserves patience through a difficult market day. The emotional challenge is that conviction can become a reason never to revisit the price. A company can keep growing while the return available to a new shareholder deteriorates because expectations have moved further. The crowd now has to decide whether a drawdown offers a better entry or reveals that too much future success was already being purchased. That question cannot be settled by admiration for the product alone."),
 part('What Is Driving It',obs('PLTR',(1,5,20),'Palantir')+"The move turns a valuation debate into an immediate position-sizing problem. Business Insider reports Andrew Left arguing that OpenAI's valuation makes Palantir look too expensive. [[n6680]] That is a named investor's comparison, not an agreed valuation model and not proof that the businesses have identical economics. But it gives sellers a legible counter-narrative: AI enthusiasm does not make every price reasonable. The closing loss must be read alongside the company's growth coverage, because the tension is between operating strength and the amount investors are willing to pay for it. A prior-session decline can change the entry price without changing either side's underlying argument."),
 part('The Social Signal',"Business Insider's earlier account quotes Left describing the valuation as 'absurd', while CNBC gives the growth case a clear visual frame. [[n5001]] [[n4968]] Those are visible competing messages, rather than an invented measure of posts or a claim about which view dominates every platform. The social mechanism is disagreement becoming identity: owners may see skepticism as another obstacle that the company will overcome, while critics see a narrative that has escaped conventional valuation. That can keep attention high even when prices fall. For a retail account, the useful discipline is to write down what operational evidence would justify a larger position and what would disprove the thesis. A falling price should prompt that review, rather than automatically count as proof of a bargain."),
 part('Historical Analog',"The comparison with Nvidia 2023 is about how an identifiable technology opportunity can attract successive waves of investors, not a prediction that another company's returns will repeat. The first wave focuses on the operating opportunity, the next on the evidence that other buyers recognise it and a later wave may focus on the discomfort of missing the move. Palantir's growth coverage supplies the present operating attraction, while the valuation criticism identifies the disagreement about the price. [[n4968]] [[n6680]] The mechanical risk is that investors continue to quote the first argument after the price has already capitalised it. The analogy should therefore encourage valuation discipline and patience with position size, not an assumption that a familiar narrative must produce a familiar outcome."),
 part('Smart Money View',"The institutional discussion is divided rather than uniformly bearish. Barron's reports a large US pension buying Palantir alongside other technology and brokerage names, while Business Insider reports a named short seller's criticism. [[n1650]] [[n5001]] A filing records ownership at a historical point and does not reveal every trade since then; a public short thesis records an argument and does not prove it correct. Both can be true without implying that the market has reached a consensus. Professionals need to distinguish agreement about the business opportunity from agreement about the return available at the prevailing price. The constructive side wants durable commercial adoption. The skeptical side wants evidence that growth can justify a demanding valuation without assuming an uninterrupted sequence of favourable developments."),
 part('When the Narrative Breaks',"The bullish story would fail if commercial adoption or cash generation no longer supported the expectations being paid for. The price could also fall while operations remain healthy if the valuation investors accept becomes less generous. Bloomberg's report of options traders buying 'disaster' protection around big technology shows that portfolio hedging is part of the surrounding market discussion; it does not establish a Palantir-specific flow. [[n6653]] Keep the scope of that evidence clear. A measured cash-equity position can survive uncertainty better than a leveraged attempt to recover a prior high. Add only when the earnings path and the valuation case are compatible, and cut exposure when defending the narrative becomes more important than evaluating the business. The original opportunity and the current entry price deserve separate decisions.") ]}
 smaller=[
 {'title':'Robinhood / HOOD — football extends the platform story','parts':[part('Why Retail Is Here',q(r'Robinhood launches NFL and college football prediction markets','retail')+obs('HOOD',(5,),'Robinhood')+"A new product makes the platform-expansion narrative concrete. The attraction for shareholders is the possibility that another form of customer activity improves engagement and revenue. Product demand is different from demand for the company's stock, so the launch should not be presented as proof that retail investors are buying HOOD."),part('Where It Breaks',q(r'Robinhood Sues Nevada, New Jersey|Robinhood.*sports betting prediction markets','retail')+"Distribution, regulation and monetisation determine whether engagement becomes durable value. An expanding menu of activities can create customer interest while also creating legal or compliance costs. Keep the equity case tied to profitable platform economics. If new products generate activity without a satisfactory return after costs, the apparently larger opportunity would not justify a larger valuation.")]},
 {'title':'Intel / INTC — confidence now needs shareholder arithmetic','parts':[part('Why Retail Is Here',q(r'Intel.s Government Equity Swap Raises Eyebrows At Bernstein, But Retail Remains Confident','retail')+"The new angle is the explicit divide between retail confidence and an analyst's questions about an equity swap. The financing story has moved beyond the comfort of a named sponsor. Existing shareholders need to know whether support improves their claim on future earnings, rather than only the company's ability to keep investing."),part('Where It Breaks',q(r'Intel gets.*SoftBank equity investment')+"Keep this as a focused carryover: the ownership terms and execution remain the tests. Support can be important and still be expensive. A plan that transfers too much upside away from current shareholders would weaken the equity thesis even if it advances a strategic industrial objective.")]},
 {'title':'Gold / GLD — policy optionality without a growth promise','parts':[part('Why Retail Is Here',q(r'Powell.s keynote speech could be next catalyst for gold and silver','retail')+obs('GC=F',(5,20),'Gold futures')+"The hook is a defensive asset whose next catalyst can come from policy rather than corporate profit. A softer financing outlook can help, while geopolitical uncertainty can create a different reason to hold it. Those mechanisms are distinct, so the position needs a clear purpose."),part('Where It Breaks',q(r'Gold prices steady as investors assess Trump.Zelenskiy meeting')+"A change in risk premium or a less favourable rate outlook can undermine the position even without a dramatic economic improvement. Futures and a bullion fund also have different trading and roll characteristics. Prefer an allocation sized to the intended hedge, and reduce it if the asset repeatedly fails to provide the protection for which it was bought. Do not turn a defensive position into an unbounded price target.")]}
 ]
 base['retail_intro']='Palantir puts the AI conviction trade through a valuation test, echoing the expectation loops familiar from Nvidia 2023, Tesla 2020 and the internet infrastructure enthusiasm of 1999.'
 base['retail']=[lead]+smaller

else:
 raise SystemExit('Own-date retail selection not written yet')
